ssion\"\n- E008: \"FIPS support posture documentation\"\n\nNow write bodies. Keep scope functional, no invented details (no specific library names, no tool names beyond what's in the strategy: \"validated cryptographic modules\", \"FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters\", \"security review board\", \"certification lab\", \"module inventory, build attestations, test results\"). Konflux is fine (canonical pipeline, referenced by implementation_type). Mention \"midstream repository under opendatahub-io\" for repo-onboarding (definition of the label).\n\nEpic E001 body:\n\n# Platform Operator midstream repository onboarding\n\n## Description\nEstablish the midstream repository for the Platform Operator component under the opendatahub-io organization so that its serving-stack images can be built, modified, and released by the platform. The Platform Operator is listed as an affected component of the FIPS compliance strategy but has no architecture-context file in the platform inventory, so it is treated as a component not yet in the platform and provisioned through the standard onboarding chain.\n\n## Scope\n- Confirm the upstream source for the Platform Operator component and whether any midstream repository already exists (the platform inventory records none; if one is found, this epic reduces to recording and validating it).\n- Create the midstream repository fork under opendatahub-io with standard branch and CI conventions.\n- Wire basic branch protection / build defaults consistent with other opendatahub-io midstream repositories.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A midstream Platform Operator repository exists under opendatahub-io (or an existing one is identified and validated), mirroring the upstream source at a pinned state.\n- The repository follows the standard opendatahub-io midstream conventions used by peer components.\n- The outcome (new repository vs. existing repository validated) is recorded so downstream build-pipeline onboarding can proceed.\n\n## HLR Traceabilit