tack images (components and the serving data plane).\n- Remediate the identified usage: rebuild/replace linked crypto libraries with validated modules, adjust base images/build flags as needed.\n- Verify the remediated images on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters (functional request path: deploy, serve inference, auth path as applicable).\n- Verify behavior is unchanged on non-FIPS clusters.\n\nACs:\n- A module inventory documents every non-validated crypto usage found in KServe serving-stack images and its remediation.\n- All KServe serving-stack images run correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters with no non-validated crypto in the request path (verified by tests on a FIPS-enabled cluster).\n- Serving behavior on non-FIPS clusters is unchanged (regression check).\n- The existing Konflux build pipeline builds the remediated images green.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-1 (primary).\n\nE005 Platform Operator FIPS remediation: similar, scoped to the Platform Operator's own images; references \"after the midstream repository exists and license validation completes\" — careful, no epic IDs; describe dependencies in plain terms. AC includes \"build pipeline green\" (Rule 25 AC since konflux-onboarding is in its chain, but no DAG edge — coupling via AC).\n\nE006 CI gating check:\n\n## Description\nA gating CI check that fails any serving-stack build which reintroduces non-validated crypto usage.\n\nScope:\n- Implement the regression check (detection of non-validated crypto usage in built images / linked libraries).\n- Wire it as a gating check in the serving-stack build pipelines (Konflux), including the Platform Operator pipeline once onboarded.\n- Define the failure output so builds identify the offending usage.\n\nACs:\n- Any serving-stack build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage fails the gating check, with output identifying the offending usage.\n- The check runs gating in the serving-stack build pipelines (Konflux), covering KServe and Platform Operator builds.\n- The check passes on the remediated images (no false positives on the FIPS-remediated state).\n\nHmm — \"including the Platform Operator pipeline once onboarded\" — coupling with konflux-onboarding expressed as AC ✓ (no edge).\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-3 (primary).\n\nE007 evidence package:\n\n## Description\nAssemble the FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package for the model serving stack — module inventory, build attestations, and test results — and submit it through the security review board to the external accredited certification laboratory.\n\nScope:\n- Compile the module inventory of cryptographic modules across the serving-stack images (from the remediation outcomes).\n- Collect signed build attestations produced by the serving-stack Konflux build pipelines (including the Platform Operator pipeline).\n- Collect FIPS-enabled-cluster test results from the remediated serving stack.\n- Assemble the package in the form the security review board and certification lab require and submit it through the review board to the lab.\n\nACs:\n- The evidence package contains the complete module inventory, signed build attestations from the serving-stack build pipelines, and FIPS-enabled-cluster test results for the remediated images.\n- The evidence package is submitted through the security review board to the external certification laboratory and its intake is confirmed (submitted and triaged).\n\nHmm, \"intake is confirmed\" — is that externally controlled? Submission is our act; confirmation of triage is the lab's. Per Step 7: \"the evidence package is submitted to the certification lab and triaged\" is a valid AC. I'll phrase: \"submitted through the security review board to the external certification laboratory (submission acknowledged)\". Keep it at engineering completion point: submission accomplished. \n\nRisks/Notes:\n- Laboratory turnaround is historically six to nine months and review-board sign-off occurs at defined milestones; both are externally controlled and