{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab31a48d81aa96aa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# RHAISTRAT-9013 — Decomposition: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n10\t\n11\tFull decomposition (no Step 0 triage): the strategy is Critical priority, spans two\n12\tcomponents (KServe, Platform Operator), and contains multiple architecturally\n13\tdistinct work streams (component onboarding, crypto remediation in two component\n14\tsets, CI enforcement, compliance evidence assembly, documentation) that warrant\n15\tseparate PR review cycles.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority | Implementation Type |\n20\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|---------------------|\n21\t| E001 | Platform Operator midstream repository onboarding | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Platform | P0 | repo-onboarding |\n22\t| E002 | Platform Operator license validation | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Platform | P0 | license-validation |\n23\t| E003 | Platform Operator Konflux build pipeline onboarding | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Platform | P0 | konflux-onboarding |\n24\t| E004 | KServe FIPS crypto remediation for serving-stack images | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 | — (general) |\n25\t| E005 | Platform Operator FIPS crypto remediation | Implementation | Platform Operator | RHOAI Platform | P0 | — (general) |\n26\t| E006 | CI gating check for FIPS crypto regressions in serving-stack builds | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P1 | — (general) |\n27\t| E007 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and submission | Implementation | KServe | RHOAI Serving | P0 | — (general) |\n28\t| E008 | FIPS support posture documentation | Implementation | Documentation | RHOAI Docs | P0 | docs-authoring |\n29\t\n30\tNotes on the epic set:\n31\t\n32\t- **New component (Platform Operator):** the architecture-context inventory\n33\t  (`.context/architecture-context/`) has a file for KServe\n34\t  (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`) but none for Platform Operator. Per the\n35\t  component-existence rule, Platform Operator is treated as not yet in the\n36\t  platform and gets the standard onboarding chain (E001 repo-onboarding +\n37\t  E002 license-validation in parallel → E003 konflux-onboarding → E005 general\n38\t  implementation, with E005 parallel to E003 per Rule 10). See Health Warnings\n39\t  for the discrepancy with the strategy's affected-components list.\n40\t- **HLR-1 spans two components:** split into E004 (KServe) and E005 (Platform\n41\t  Operator) by the component/team boundary rule — an architectural split, not a\n42\t  priority split.\n43\t- **E008 priority:** the docs-authoring epic's priority derives from the\n44\t  strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0) via the docs-authoring\n45\t  priority exception, not from HLR-4's P2 label.\n46\t\n47\t## Investigation Analysis\n48\t\n49\tNo Investigation epics. The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None on\n50\tscope\"; there are no conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks table entries\n51\traising structural unknowns. The one flagged item — certification-lab scheduling\n52\tand review-board cadence being externally controlled — is a schedule constraint,\n53\tnot an unknown that changes which epics exist or what they do; it is recorded as\n54\tRisks/Notes on E007 and in Health Warnings. The crypto module inventory\n55\t(\"several images\" link non-validated crypto) determines remediation volume\n56\tinside E004/E005, not the existence or shape of the epics — it is captured as\n57\tan acceptance criterion on those epics, not an Investigation.\n58\t\n59\t## Dependency DAG\n60\t\n61\t```mermaid\n62\tgraph TD\n63\t    E001 --> E003\n64\t    E001 --> E005\n65\t    E002 --> E005\n66\t    E004 --> E007\n67\t    E005 --> E007\n68\t    E001 --> E008\n69\t    E002 --> E008\n70\t    E003 --> E008\n71\t    E004 --> E008\n72\t    E005 --> E008\n73\t    E006 --> E008\n74\t    E007 --> E008\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\t(E001 repo onboarding, E002 license validation, E003 Konflux onboarding,\n78\tE004 KServe FIPS remediation, E005 Platform Operator FIPS remediation,\n79\tE006 CI gating check, E007 evidence package, E008 docs. Arrows point from\n80\tdependency to dependent.)\n81\t\n82\tCritical path: E001 → E005 → E007 → E008 (length 4), consistent with a\n83\tmedium strategy with a new component (heuristic 4-5).\n84\t\n85\t## DAG Justification\n86\t\n87\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n88\t|------|------|-----------|\n89\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 5 | repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding always serial; the pipeline needs the repository. |\n90\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 6 | repo-onboarding → general implementation of the onboarded component serial; remediation code needs the midstream repository. Does not block KServe work (E004). |\n91\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 9 | license-validation → general implementation serial; if licenses fail, the dependency set changes and the remediation approach changes. |\n92\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | The evidence package documents the remediated KServe images and contains their FIPS-enabled-cluster test results; it cannot be assembled before the remediation that produces those artifacts. |\n93\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | Same for the Platform Operator: the package includes its remediation outcomes and test results. |\n94\t| E001…E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring exception). |\n95\t\n96\tMandated parallel pairs — no edge in either direction, coupling expressed as\n97\tacceptance-criterion gates:\n98\t\n99\t| Non-edge | Rule | Coupling expressed as |\n100\t|----------|------|----------------------|\n101\t| E002 ∥ E001 | Rule 7 | license-validation and repo-onboarding have independent inputs; both start immediately. |\n102\t| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 8 | pipeline config does not depend on the license verdict. |\n103\t| E003 ∥ E005 | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding runs parallel to the general implementation. Coupling is the \"build pipeline green\" AC on E005 — never a DAG edge. |\n104\t| E003 ∥ E007 | Rule 10 | The evidence epic consumes the pipeline's attestations as content: AC on E007 (\"signed build attestations from the serving-stack build pipelines, including the Platform Operator pipeline, are included\"), not an edge. |\n105\t| E003 ∥ E006 | Rule 10 | The CI check gates builds in the pipelines: AC on E006 (\"the check runs gating in the serving-stack build pipelines, including the Platform Operator pipeline once onboarded\"), not an edge. |\n106\t| E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14 | Different components/repos, no shared artifacts. |\n107\t| E004 ∥ E006 | Rule 15 | Same repo area family but different work products (image remediation vs. CI check); merge-conflict coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n108\t| E006 ∥ E004/E005/E007 | Rules 14/15 | The CI check is developed independently of the remediation it protects; no shared artifact. |\n109\t\n110\t## Component Mapping Notes\n111\t\n112\t- All `component` values are verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n113\t  (KServe, Platform Operator, Documentation).\n114\t- E006 (CI gating check) and E007 (evidence package) span both strategy\n115\t  components (KServe and Platform Operator). Each is assigned the single\n116\t  most-relevant canonical component — KServe, the core of the model serving\n117\t  stack whose builds and images the work targets — with the span noted here.\n118\t- E008 is assigned the canonical `Documentation` component per the\n119\t  docs-authoring implementation type.\n120\t\n121\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n122\t\n123\t| HLR | Priority | Epics | Notes |\n124\t|-----|----------|-------|-------|\n125\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E004, E005 | Split by component boundary (KServe vs. Platform Operator) — an architectural split mandated by the epic boundary rules, not a priority split. E001–E003 are enabling epics for the Platform Operator share. |\n126\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E007 | Evidence package assembly and submission through the security review board to the certification lab. |\n127\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E006 | CI gating check for FIPS regressions across serving-stack builds. |\n128\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E008 | docs-authoring epic; its own priority is P0 by the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Critical), so the P2 label on the HLR reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. No bundling analysis needed — each HLR maps to its own epic(s) at a single priority level. |\n129\t\n130\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n131\t\n132\t## Health Warnings\n133\t\n134\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no\n135\t   Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance\n136\t   criteria, and no NFRs; the Affected Components section is a bare list with\n137\t   no per-component change description or owner. Team assignments are\n138\t   inferred: RHOAI Serving from the KServe architecture-context file;\n139\t   RHOAI Platform and RHOAI Docs are inferred generic assignments — verify\n140\t   with the strategy author before scheduling.\n141\t2. **Platform Operator inventory discrepancy:** the strategy lists Platform\n142\t   Operator as an affected (implicitly existing) component, but the\n143\t   architecture-context inventory has no file for it. Per the\n144\t   component-existence rule it is treated as not in the platform and given the\n145\t   full onboarding chain (E001–E003). If the component already has a midstream\n146\t   repository and pipeline (inventory gap rather than a genuinely new\n147\t   component), E001 reduces to validating the existing repository and E003 to\n148\t   confirming pipeline onboarding — the DAG shape is unchanged either way.\n149\t   Human verification recommended.\n150\t3. **External schedule gates:** certification-laboratory turnaround\n151\t   (historically six to nine months) and security-review-board cadence are\n152\t   externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort\n153\t   (strategy problem statement / open-questions note). Recorded as Risks/Notes\n154\t   on E007 — deliberately NOT as acceptance criteria. See Tiered Delivery.\n155\t4. **Docs epic priority:** E008 carries P0 via the docs-authoring priority\n156\t   exception (strategy priority Critical → P0) although HLR-4 is P2. This is\n157\t   by design, not a priority inversion.\n158\t5. **E004 size watch item with re-split trigger:** the strategy says\n159\t   \"several images\" link non-validated crypto but names none, so remediation\n160\t   volume is unknown until the module inventory is complete. E004 is kept as\n161\t   one logical change (a single mechanical remediation pass over the KServe\n162\t   image set — splitting before the inventory exists would fragment the audit\n163\t   and duplicate the inventory work). Re-split trigger: if the completed\n164\t   module inventory shows more KServe images needing remediation than fit in\n165\t   one sprint, re-split E004 by image group (serving data plane vs. control\n166\t   plane), each split carrying its slice of the inventory.\n167\t\n168\t## Tiered Delivery\n169\t\n170\tThe external certification dependency has uncertain timing, and the strategy\n171\tcan deliver partial value without it (Step 5.5 test passes):\n172\t\n173\t- **Tier 1 — independent value (E001–E006):** the model serving stack runs\n174\t  correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters using validated crypto modules\n175\t  (HLR-1), with CI enforcement preventing regressions (HLR-3). Public-sector\n176\t  deployments on FIPS-enabled clusters get a working serving stack before\n177\t  certification completes.\n178\t- **Tier 2 — externally gated (E007, then E008):** the compliance evidence\n179\t  package (HLR-2) is assembled once remediation completes and submitted\n180\t  through the security review board to the certification lab; certified\n181\t  compliance arrives on the lab's schedule (six to nine months historical\n182\t  turnaround), which dominates the Tier 2 timeline. The documentation epic\n183\t  follows all implementations per Rule 11 and reflects the implemented\n184\t  posture.\n185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac2dc8c8f1dff594","agent_id":"af9e2b0c7e49be959","session_id":"29cb84a9-828d-4dcd-a97c-1ceca07010da","uuid":"e4ba4d13-3588-4c69-9f34-ce82bdaf63d7","timestamp":"2026-10-23T17:57:58.602Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab31a48d81aa96aa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
